  <data name="Info_About_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>About</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Info_About_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Vacuo is a maintenance tool for Windows that looks after your disk, memory and cluttered folders from one panel. Its name is Latin for "in the void".

Vacuo measures honestly: it shows caches with what it costs to rebuild them, and never fakes a "RAM clean-up". It does nothing without your approval, and everything it moves can be put back from the Journal.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Info_Privacy_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Privacy</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Info_Privacy_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value># In short
Vacuo asks for no account, and has no telemetry, analytics or ads. Your information stays on this computer.

# Internet
Vacuo's only network connection is the update check. When the panel opens, at most every 6 hours, and when you press "Check now" in Settings, it reads the list of releases on GitHub. If you press Update, it downloads the new version's setup and its SHA-256 digest, also from GitHub. No account, identity or usage information is sent; the only thing GitHub learns is the name "Vacuo/version" (and, as with any connection, your IP address). With "Check for updates" turned off in Settings, Vacuo never goes online. The tray icon never goes online.

# Kept on this computer
In the %LOCALAPPDATA%\Vacuo folder: your settings (language and update choice), the Journal (path, size and time of each item moved), the results of administrator clean-ups on the Windows screen (size and number of files deleted), the time of the last update check (update.json) and the downloaded setup (the updates folder; removed on the first start after updating). If "Start with Windows" is on, only Vacuo's own startup entry is written.

# Kept in memory only
The results of the disk scan, the duplicate search and the list of installed programs, and, for the Memory screen, when each program was last in front. None of this is written to disk; it is gone when Vacuo closes.

# What is read
Vacuo reads the names, sizes and dates of files; to compare contents in the duplicate search it computes a digest (SHA-256). It only reads the installed programs from the registry. It reads battery information from the driver; the battery's name and serial number are never asked for.</value>
  </data>
